README

This sample program uses a Raspberry Pi as a raw SOEM (Simple Open EtherCAT
Master) master, which controls a single Elmo Platinum servo drive directly
over CoE (CANopen over EtherCAT), with explicit control over every AL state
transition. It is console-only: all status and debug output goes to the
serial interface, there is no web page and no second Ethernet port.

	+-------------------+  eth0 (raw EtherCAT frames)  +--------------+
	|  Raspberry Pi     |----------------------------->| Elmo Platinum|
	|  (Circle + SOEM)  |                              | drive        |
	+-------------------+                              +--------------+

Unlike sample/48-elmoweb, this sample does NOT use the JSD library. JSD's
jsd_init() brings the bus from INIT to OPERATIONAL internally and does not
expose the individual state transitions. This sample instead calls the SOEM
"ecx_*" API directly, the same way sample/47-ethercatweb does, so that the
state machine sequence required by this application is explicit in the code:

	INIT -> PRE_OP -> (configure the drive via CoE/SDO) -> SAFE_OP
	     -> wait 5 ms -> OPERATIONAL -> CiA 402 enable sequence

	!!! THIS PROGRAM CAN MOVE A MOTOR (a small demo move by default). !!!
	!!! READ THE SAFETY SECTION BELOW BEFORE YOU CONNECT A DRIVE.     !!!

Required hardware:

	* A Raspberry Pi with an on-board Ethernet controller (e.g. CM5)
	* One Elmo Platinum drive, connected to the on-board Ethernet port
	* A serial USB adapter (3.3V level) or a Raspberry Pi Debug Probe, to
	  read the debug output (see "DEBUG OUTPUT" below)

Do not connect the EtherCAT port to your office network. The EtherCAT master
sends raw Ethernet frames to this port and there is no IP stack behind it.


BUILD

Download and build the SOEM library first, as described in the file
addon/soem/README:

	cd addon/soem
	git clone https://github.com/OpenEtherCATsociety/SOEM.git
	make

Then build this sample as usual:

	cd sample/49-elmoplatinum
	make

If the library libsoem.a has not been built, the build of this sample is
skipped with a message, so that a build of all samples does not fail.


BEFORE YOU START: SAFETY

	ELMO_ENABLE_DEMO_MOTION		1 by default (see elmoplatinum.h)
	ELMO_DEMO_VELOCITY_CPS		1000 counts/s (intentionally very slow)
	ELMO_DEMO_MOVE_MS		2000 ms
	ELMO_MAX_PROFILE_VELOCITY	5000 counts/s (object 0x607F)
	ELMO_PROFILE_ACCEL/DECEL	10000 counts/s^2 (objects 0x6083/0x6084)

Once the drive reaches "Operation enabled", this sample runs a single,
bounded demo motion (a short, slow Profile Velocity move, then it commands
velocity 0 and stops - it is not a continuous jog). ADAPT THESE VALUES TO
YOUR MOTOR before you connect one, or set ELMO_ENABLE_DEMO_MOTION to 0 in
elmoplatinum.h, if you only want to test the EtherCAT/CiA 402 state machine
without commanding any motion at all.

	THERE IS NO WEB INTERFACE AND NO WATCHDOG-DRIVEN INTERLOCK HERE, since
	there is no remote control surface. The demo motion is one-shot,
	bounded in time and speed, and disableable at compile time.

This sample has never been tested against real Elmo hardware.


WHAT IT DOES

The on-board Ethernet device (net device 0) is used for EtherCAT. The class
CElmoPlatinumMaster (see elmoplatinum.h/.cpp) runs the master in its own
task:

1. ecx_init() opens the net device, ecx_config_init() brings every slave from
   INIT to PRE_OP. The PRE_OP state is explicitly verified and logged before
   any configuration is attempted.
2. While the slave is in PRE_OP, its identity is read (SII vendor ID/product
   code, CoE objects 0x1008/0x1009/0x100A) and logged, and the drive is then
   configured via CoE/SDO writes (ConfigureDrive() in elmoplatinum.cpp):
	- RxPDO 0x1600: Controlword (0x6040) + Target velocity (0x60FF)
	- TxPDO 0x1A00: Statusword (0x6041) + Velocity actual value (0x606C)
	- PDO assignment via 0x1C12/0x1C13
	- Mode of operation (0x6060) = 3 (Profile Velocity)
	- Conservative motion limits (0x607F, 0x6083, 0x6084)
   These are standard CiA 402 (DS402) objects, not Elmo-specific ones.
3. ecx_config_map_group() maps the process data just configured, which also
   drives the PRE_OP -> SAFE_OP transition. This is explicitly verified and
   logged.
4. The master then explicitly waits 5 ms (SAFE_OP_TO_OPERATIONAL_DELAY_US in
   elmoplatinum.cpp) before requesting the OPERATIONAL state, exchanging
   process data continuously while it waits for the transition to complete
   (some drives require this).
5. Once OPERATIONAL, the cyclic process data loop decodes the drive's
   statusword (object 0x6041) into its CiA 402 state and drives the standard
   enable sequence (Shutdown -> Switch On -> Enable Operation) by writing the
   controlword (object 0x6040). Once "Operation enabled" is reached, the
   bounded demo motion described above runs once.
6. The system log shows the master and drive state every 10 seconds too.


DEBUG OUTPUT

The debug messages (the Circle system log) are written to the serial
interface, so that the application can be troubleshooted while it is
running. On the Raspberry Pi 5 the dedicated 3-pin UART connector is used,
served by uart10 (serial device 10 in Circle, "ttyS11"). Other Raspberry Pi
models use the UART on GPIO14/15. The settings are 115200 baud, 8 data bits,
no parity, 1 stop bit, defined in kernel.h (SERIAL_DEVICE_NUMBER and
SERIAL_BAUD_RATE).

Connect a serial USB adapter (3.3V level) or a Raspberry Pi Debug Probe to
the UART connector and open a terminal program (e.g. putty, minicom or
"screen /dev/ttyUSB0 115200") on your development machine.

The following options can be added to cmdline.txt on the SD card:

	loglevel=4	log level: 0 (panic) to 4 (debug, this is the default)
	logtarget=...	write the log messages to another registered device

The serial driver is used in interrupt driven mode, so that writing a debug
message does not stall the EtherCAT cycle until the characters have been
sent. The messages are buffered instead; increase the baud rate or the
system option SERIAL_BUF_SIZE (see include/circle/sysconfig.h), if a lot of
messages are logged in a short time.

The output looks like this:

	00:00:02.14 kernel: Compile time: Aug 17 2026 09:12:33
	00:00:02.15 elmo: Elmo Platinum master starting on eth0 (cycle time 4000 us)
	00:00:02.31 elmo: 1 slave(s) found, slave 1 is in PRE_OP, configuring the drive
	00:00:02.31 elmo: slave[1] "EPD" vendor 0x9A product 0x100002: Elmo Platinum drive
	00:00:02.35 elmo: Drive configured: Profile Velocity mode, max velocity 5000 counts/s
	00:00:02.40 elmo: Slave reached SAFE_OP (6 byte(s) out, 6 byte(s) in, expected WKC 3)
	00:00:02.40 elmo: Waiting 5000 us before requesting OPERATIONAL
	00:00:02.60 elmo: Slave is operational
	00:00:02.60 elmo: Drive state: Switch on disabled (statusword 0x0250)
	00:00:02.61 elmo: Drive state: Ready to switch on (statusword 0x0231)
	00:00:02.61 elmo: Drive state: Switched on (statusword 0x0233)
	00:00:02.62 elmo: Drive state: Operation enabled (statusword 0x0237)
	00:00:03.12 elmo: Starting demo motion: target velocity 1000 counts/s for 2000 ms
	00:00:05.12 elmo: Demo motion done, actual velocity 998 counts/s
	00:00:12.15 kernel: EtherCAT: OPERATIONAL, drive: Operation enabled, WKC 3 of 3, ...


NOTES

The scheduler of Circle is cooperative and non-preemptive. The 5 ms wait
between SAFE_OP and OPERATIONAL, and the cyclic process data exchange, use
CScheduler::usSleep(), which yields to other tasks, so other work in the
application is not blocked while waiting.

If the link is down or the slave is not found, the master retries every
5 seconds. If the connection to the slave is lost while OPERATIONAL, the
master re-scans and re-configures the bus from PRE_OP again.

This sample assumes a single slave (slave 1) on the bus and does not scan
for or identify multiple drives, unlike sample/48-elmoweb.
